        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 1930,
            "sid": 3,
            "position_x": 1019,
            "position_y": 13,
            "width": 768,
            "height": 996,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Cuts a rectangle out of a 2-D array or an image, given its left, top, right and bottom edges.\n\nIt is the picture-shaped way of selecting: four numbers you can put on sliders, rather than a slice to type. Use it to keep a region of a camera frame, a window of a depth map, or the corner of a matrix.\n\nTHE EDGES:\nleft and top are the first column and row KEPT. right and bottom are the first column and row LEFT OUT - so the result is (bottom - top) rows by (right - left) columns. np.crop 2 3 7 8 keeps rows 3 to 7 and columns 2 to 6: 5 x 5. This is the same as np.[] 3:8,2:7 - note the order differs: np.crop is x first (left, top...), the slice is rows first.\n\nOnly the first two dimensions are cut, so an image shaped (height, width, 3) keeps all three colours. A 1-D array is ignored: nothing is sent.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.crop <left: int> <top: int> <right: int> <bottom: int>\nDefaults: 0 0 256 256.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nnp.crop 2 3 7 8\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nimage in:\nThe array (a list is converted). Sends the cropped piece on arrival. This is the ONLY inlet that makes the node send.\n\nleft / top / right / bottom (drag ints, also inlets):\nThe edges. Changing them does NOT re-crop the last array - the next one to arrive uses them. In the demo a metro resends the source 10 times a second, so the crop follows your drags.\nOut-of-range edges are pulled inside the array before cutting: a right or bottom beyond the array means its far edge, and the rectangle is never less than one cell. The boxes keep showing what you typed - right 256 on a 10-column array crops at column 10.\n\nuncrop (button):\nSets the edges to the whole array (0, 0, width, height) of the last array received. It does not send by itself; the next array comes out whole.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nout array:\nThe rectangle, as a new contiguous array.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe left heat_map is the source, each cell printing its own number - tens digit the row, units digit the column. Drag the edges on np.crop and the right heat_map shows the rectangle kept, still numbered, so you can read which rows and columns survived. np.shape gives its size.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.[] selects by a typed slice and can also step, reverse and pick lists of rows. np.clip is a different thing despite the similar name: it limits VALUES, keeping the shape. np.edit writes into a region. pc_crop crops a point cloud by a 3-D box.",
                    "value_type": "string"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 980,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "string"
                }
            },
            "text": "Cuts a rectangle out of a 2-D array or an image, given its left, top, right and bottom edges.\n\nIt is the picture-shaped way of selecting: four numbers you can put on sliders, rather than a slice to type. Use it to keep a region of a camera frame, a window of a depth map, or the corner of a matrix.\n\nTHE EDGES:\nleft and top are the first column and row KEPT. right and bottom are the first column and row LEFT OUT - so the result is (bottom - top) rows by (right - left) columns. np.crop 2 3 7 8 keeps rows 3 to 7 and columns 2 to 6: 5 x 5. This is the same as np.[] 3:8,2:7 - note the order differs: np.crop is x first (left, top...), the slice is rows first.\n\nOnly the first two dimensions are cut, so an image shaped (height, width, 3) keeps all three colours. A 1-D array is ignored: nothing is sent.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.crop <left: int> <top: int> <right: int> <bottom: int>\nDefaults: 0 0 256 256.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nnp.crop 2 3 7 8\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nimage in:\nThe array (a list is converted). Sends the cropped piece on arrival. This is the ONLY inlet that makes the node send.\n\nleft / top / right / bottom (drag ints, also inlets):\nThe edges. Changing them does NOT re-crop the last array - the next one to arrive uses them. In the demo a metro resends the source 10 times a second, so the crop follows your drags.\nOut-of-range edges are pulled inside the array before cutting: a right or bottom beyond the array means its far edge, and the rectangle is never less than one cell. The boxes keep showing what you typed - right 256 on a 10-column array crops at column 10.\n\nuncrop (button):\nSets the edges to the whole array (0, 0, width, height) of the last array received. It does not send by itself; the next array comes out whole.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nout array:\nThe rectangle, as a new contiguous array.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe left heat_map is the source, each cell printing its own number - tens digit the row, units digit the column. Drag the edges on np.crop and the right heat_map shows the rectangle kept, still numbered, so you can read which rows and columns survived. np.shape gives its size.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.[] selects by a typed slice and can also step, reverse and pick lists of rows. np.clip is a different thing despite the similar name: it limits VALUES, keeping the shape. np.edit writes into a region. pc_crop crops a point cloud by a 3-D box."
        },
